Parent (28c4a33b): good find. Before I rule on the seam, answer one read-only question. No code yet; keep your uncommitted worktree as it is.
JVM method calls get overload and applicability handling through the shared call-applicability machinery (CallApplicabilityAdapter and ConversionEvidence in fact_resolution/call_applicability.rs). C# has no such adapter, yet the shared path answers C#'s M() method groups as complete. Map, with file:line at origin/master:
(1) where the shared native definition path decides a Value lookup's targets and completion for a call-position name, and where call applicability is consulted for languages that have an adapter;
(2) what happens for a language WITHOUT an adapter: does the shared path skip applicability and still mark the answer Complete? That would be the general fail-open defect;
(3) the minimal seam for each option:
   (A) a shared fail-closed rule: if the language supplies no applicability adapter and a selected target is a callable in call position, combine a registered incomplete reason, without per-language code;
   (B) a C#-only answer-level hook on DemandInstall, with a default no-op, called after the targets are known.
Say which other languages would also be affected by (A) (Go, Rust, Python, C++?), and whether each already has applicability. Hand back MAP.md with your recommendation; I'll rule.
